1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,790
A passenger sleeps on a moving train.

2
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:04,944
Is she still, or is she moving?

3
00:00:05,344 --> 00:00:06,623
You sit at your desk.

4
00:00:06,623 --> 00:00:07,523
Still?

5
00:00:07,110 --> 00:00:09,085
But the Earth under you is spinning.

6
00:00:09,535 --> 00:00:13,652
And Aarav rides past the college
gate with his bag on his back.

7
00:00:14,002 --> 00:00:17,032
Aarav says, my bag has
not moved one centimetre.

8
00:00:17,032 --> 00:00:20,209
Diya, at the gate, says,
it just shot past me.

9
00:00:20,609 --> 00:00:21,922
They are both right.

10
00:00:21,922 --> 00:00:25,059
By the end of this video,
you will see exactly why.

11
00:00:25,709 --> 00:00:28,739
To describe motion, we must
say where something is.

12
00:00:29,089 --> 00:00:31,681
But where is always
measured from somewhere.

13
00:00:31,681 --> 00:00:32,702
From the gate?

14
00:00:32,702 --> 00:00:33,602
From Aarav?

15
00:00:33,933 --> 00:00:36,210
That somewhere is a reference point.

16
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:38,148
Physicists call it the origin.

17
00:00:38,548 --> 00:00:42,698
Add a direction that counts as
positive, and a clock to read the time.

18
00:00:43,098 --> 00:00:46,894
Origin, direction and clock
together make a frame of reference.

19
00:00:47,544 --> 00:00:51,577
Let's run Aarav's ride on our
motion notebook, in two frames.

20
00:00:51,927 --> 00:00:53,558
Frame one is the ground.

21
00:00:53,558 --> 00:00:56,727
The origin is at the gate,
and right is positive.

22
00:00:57,127 --> 00:00:59,522
Aarav rides at five metres per second.

23
00:00:59,522 --> 00:01:03,306
Every second, his bag gets five
metres further from the gate.

24
00:01:03,706 --> 00:01:06,518
Frame two rides along with the bicycle.

25
00:01:06,518 --> 00:01:08,611
Its origin is Aarav himself.

26
00:01:09,011 --> 00:01:11,731
In this frame, the bag stays at zero.

27
00:01:11,731 --> 00:01:13,599
Its position never changes.

28
00:01:13,999 --> 00:01:15,140
Slow-mo replay.

29
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:16,943
Same bag, same moment.

30
00:01:16,943 --> 00:01:19,023
From the gate, ten metres.

31
00:01:19,023 --> 00:01:20,495
From Aarav, zero.

32
00:01:20,995 --> 00:01:22,313
Look at the graph.

33
00:01:22,313 --> 00:01:24,702
In the ground frame, a rising line.

34
00:01:24,702 --> 00:01:27,043
In the bicycle frame, a flat line.

35
00:01:27,693 --> 00:01:30,372
So let's reason it out,
one link at a time.

36
00:01:30,722 --> 00:01:33,695
Moving means your position
changes with time.

37
00:01:34,095 --> 00:01:36,741
But position is measured from an origin.

38
00:01:36,741 --> 00:01:39,744
Pick a new origin, and
the positions change.

39
00:01:40,144 --> 00:01:44,359
So the same bag can be moving in
one frame and at rest in another.

40
00:01:44,359 --> 00:01:46,253
Rest and motion are relative.

41
00:01:46,903 --> 00:01:49,669
Now let's connect the
two frames with one rule.

42
00:01:49,669 --> 00:01:51,925
Take the water bottle on the bicycle.

43
00:01:52,275 --> 00:01:53,175
Step one.

44
00:01:53,149 --> 00:01:56,059
The bottle's position from the gate is x.

45
00:01:56,059 --> 00:01:58,639
Its position from Aarav is x prime.

46
00:01:59,389 --> 00:02:00,289
Step two.

47
00:02:00,289 --> 00:02:05,448
Aarav's own position from the gate is
V times t, because he rides at speed

48
00:02:05,448 --> 00:02:06,348
V.

49
00:02:06,648 --> 00:02:07,677
Step three.

50
00:02:07,677 --> 00:02:09,926
Lengths along a line add up.

51
00:02:09,926 --> 00:02:12,365
So x equals x prime plus V t.

52
00:02:13,115 --> 00:02:14,360
Check the units.

53
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:18,197
Metres equal metres, plus
metres per second times seconds.

54
00:02:18,197 --> 00:02:19,391
That is metres.

55
00:02:19,391 --> 00:02:20,291
It fits.

56
00:02:20,821 --> 00:02:21,900
Remember this.

57
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:25,482
Rest and motion always depend
on the frame you choose.

58
00:02:26,182 --> 00:02:27,248
Side by side.

59
00:02:27,248 --> 00:02:31,909
In the ground frame, the bag reads
zero, five, ten, fifteen metres.

60
00:02:32,309 --> 00:02:36,231
In the bicycle frame, it
reads zero, zero, zero, zero.

61
00:02:36,231 --> 00:02:37,959
Same bag, same seconds.

62
00:02:38,559 --> 00:02:39,666
Now you try.

63
00:02:39,666 --> 00:02:43,771
In Aarav's frame, is Diya at
the gate moving or at rest?

64
00:02:43,771 --> 00:02:45,146
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:48,346 --> 00:02:49,246
Moving!

66
00:02:48,995 --> 00:02:53,449
Her distance from Aarav grows by five
metres every second, and she falls

67
00:02:53,449 --> 00:02:54,354
behind him.

68
00:02:54,354 --> 00:02:56,723
In his frame, she moves backwards.

69
00:02:57,373 --> 00:02:58,556
One more thing.

70
00:02:58,556 --> 00:03:02,314
The origin and the positive
direction are your own choice.

71
00:03:02,664 --> 00:03:05,797
Keep the gate as the origin,
and right as positive.

72
00:03:05,797 --> 00:03:09,584
A dog sitting three metres to the
right is at plus three metres.

73
00:03:09,984 --> 00:03:13,323
Now the dog moves to three
metres left of the gate.

74
00:03:13,323 --> 00:03:14,810
What is its position?

75
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:16,045
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:19,245 --> 00:03:20,671
Minus three metres!

77
00:03:20,671 --> 00:03:22,904
Left of the origin is negative.

78
00:03:22,904 --> 00:03:25,353
The minus sign tells you the side.

79
00:03:26,003 --> 00:03:27,739
Now, one worked example.

80
00:03:28,089 --> 00:03:32,099
Aarav passes the gate at time
zero, at five metres per second.

81
00:03:32,099 --> 00:03:34,783
His bottle is half a metre ahead of him.

82
00:03:34,783 --> 00:03:38,101
Where is the bottle from
the gate after six seconds?

83
00:03:38,601 --> 00:03:39,774
Use the rule.

84
00:03:39,774 --> 00:03:41,913
x equals x prime plus V t.

85
00:03:42,663 --> 00:03:45,587
Here x prime is zero point five metres.

86
00:03:45,587 --> 00:03:47,779
V is five metres per second.

87
00:03:47,779 --> 00:03:49,524
And t is six seconds.

88
00:03:50,274 --> 00:03:52,113
V t is five times six.

89
00:03:52,113 --> 00:03:53,823
That is thirty metres.

90
00:03:54,573 --> 00:03:57,086
So x is zero point five plus thirty.

91
00:03:57,086 --> 00:04:00,564
The bottle is thirty point
five metres from the gate.

92
00:04:01,214 --> 00:04:02,679
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:03,029 --> 00:04:07,752
Many students say the ground is the
true frame, so the bag is really moving.

94
00:04:08,102 --> 00:04:10,089
But no frame is the true one.

95
00:04:10,089 --> 00:04:12,113
From the gate, the bag moves.

96
00:04:12,113 --> 00:04:13,651
From Aarav, it rests.

97
00:04:13,651 --> 00:04:16,397
Both are correct, once you name the frame.

98
00:04:17,047 --> 00:04:18,532
Where is this asked?

99
00:04:18,532 --> 00:04:23,056
As concept questions on rest and
motion, and as the first step of every

100
00:04:23,056 --> 00:04:24,680
relative motion problem.

101
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:26,503
Exam level now.

102
00:04:26,503 --> 00:04:30,454
A truck passes a signal post
at fifteen metres per second.

103
00:04:30,454 --> 00:04:33,181
A box sits four metres behind the cab.

104
00:04:33,181 --> 00:04:35,627
Find both positions at two seconds.

105
00:04:35,627 --> 00:04:36,903
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:42,303 --> 00:04:43,612
The answer is A.

107
00:04:43,612 --> 00:04:46,575
Twenty six metres, and
minus thirty metres.

108
00:04:47,075 --> 00:04:50,926
For the box, x equals minus
four, plus fifteen times two.

109
00:04:50,926 --> 00:04:52,726
That is twenty six metres.

110
00:04:53,226 --> 00:04:56,632
For the post, x prime equals x minus V t.

111
00:04:56,632 --> 00:04:58,142
Zero minus thirty.

112
00:04:58,142 --> 00:04:59,766
Minus thirty metres.

113
00:05:00,366 --> 00:05:01,825
Here is your recap.

114
00:05:01,825 --> 00:05:03,324
Screenshot this card.

115
00:05:06,524 --> 00:05:09,522
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

116
00:05:09,922 --> 00:05:10,822
One.

117
00:05:10,421 --> 00:05:14,421
How can the same bag be at rest
and moving at the same time?

118
00:05:15,321 --> 00:05:16,221
Two.

119
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:18,620
What three things make
a frame of reference?

120
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:20,420
Three.

121
00:05:20,134 --> 00:05:23,146
A ball sits four metres
left of your origin.

122
00:05:23,146 --> 00:05:24,697
What is its position?

123
00:05:25,797 --> 00:05:30,864
Next time: Aarav rides to the shop and
back, yet his displacement is zero.

124
00:05:30,864 --> 00:05:31,764
How?

125
00:05:31,382 --> 00:05:34,143
We find out in position and displacement.
